-- status  : Definition
-- author  : @iris
-- created : 2026-10-31T11:32:19.892735+00:00
-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/a98bb7d9-ec4b-4e1f-bfcb-be73f257ae42
-- title:
--   $f$ is $L_U$ smooth along $U$ (Assumption 1, A.1)
-- statement:
--   Let $f:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R$, let $U\in\mathbb R^{n\times p}$ and $L_U\in\mathbb R$. The function $f$ is **$L_U$ smooth along $U$** if its gradient is Lipschitz continuous in the subspace generated by the columns of $U$, in the sense
--
--   $$
--   \|U^T(\nabla f(x+Uh)-\nabla f(x))\|\le L_U\|h\|\qquad\forall x\in\mathbb R^n,\ h\in\mathbb R^p .
--   $$
--
--   This is display (2) of Assumption 1 (A.1). It is the only smoothness of $f$ that the analysis of SCPG uses.
--
--   **Formalization Note** All norms are Euclidean (`EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)`, `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin p)`), and $U$ acts as the linear map `Matrix.toEuclideanLin U`. Differentiability of $f$ is a separate hypothesis wherever the predicate is used.
-- source:
--   Necoara & Chorobura, Efficiency of stochastic coordinate proximal gradient methods on nonseparable composite optimization, arXiv:2104.13370v3, Assumption 1 (A.1), display (2), p.4

import Mathlib

open Matrix

namespace StochCoordProx

/-- **`f` is `L_U` smooth along `U`** (Assumption 1, A.1, display (2)): for every `x ∈ ℝⁿ` and
every `h ∈ ℝᵖ`,
`‖Uᵀ (∇f(x + U h) − ∇f(x))‖ ≤ L_U ‖h‖`,
i.e. the gradient of `f` is Lipschitz in the subspace generated by the columns of `U`.

Necoara & Chorobura, *Efficiency of stochastic coordinate proximal gradient methods on
nonseparable composite optimization*, arXiv:2104.13370v3, Assumption 1 (A.1), display (2), p.4.

**Formalization Note.** `ℝⁿ` and `ℝᵖ` are `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)` and
`EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin p)`, so every norm is the Euclidean norm. The matrix
`U ∈ ℝ^{n×p}` acts as the linear map `Matrix.toEuclideanLin U : ℝᵖ → ℝⁿ`, and `Uᵀ` as
`Matrix.toEuclideanLin Uᵀ : ℝⁿ → ℝᵖ`. `∇f` is Mathlib's `gradient f`. Differentiability of `f`
is not part of this predicate; it is a separate hypothesis wherever the predicate is used. -/
def IsSmoothAlong {n p : ℕ} (f : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → ℝ) (U : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin p) ℝ)
    (LU : ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ (x : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)) (h : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin p)),
    ‖Matrix.toEuclideanLin Uᵀ (gradient f (x + Matrix.toEuclideanLin U h) - gradient f x)‖ ≤
      LU * ‖h‖

end StochCoordProx


